ans are compact disorder names (*Coats disease*, *Sjogren-Larsson syndrome*, *Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein*) or Modifier uses. Two control documents also contain the same \"deficiency/deficient + of + molecule\" construction, and gold leaves it unannotated:\n- 10484772: \"...results in a **deficiency of norrin** (the protein product of the NDP gene)\" has no annotation.\n- 10577908: \"...**deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase**\" and \"**severe reduction of FALDH enzyme** catalytic activity\" have no annotation.\n\n**Isolate the trigger:** The word \"deficiency\" resembles a disease suffix, as in \"G6PD deficiency\" and \"Coats disease,\" so lexical priors push it toward SpecificDisease. The model then extends the span rightward through the PP to capture the gene or protein name, which looks like part of the disorder's name. The positional cue is that the molecular entity is post-posed and introduced by of/in, rather than premodifying a lexicalized disease term.\n\n**Hypothesis check:**\n- Example 1 (in-PP, mechanistic \"responsible for enzyme deficiency in G6PD A-\") fits.\n- Example 2 (causal \"caused by a deficiency of…\") fits. The true disease here is the preceding *phenylketonuria*, and the span describes the mechanism.\n- Examples 3 and 4 (of-PP naming a complement component or gene) fit.\n\nThe trigger explains all four, but the rule needs one refinement. The same doc 1303173 correctly labels the pre-nominal compound \"G6PD deficiency\" (SpecificDisease) and \"G6PD deficient\" (Modifier). So \"deficiency\" is not disqualifying on its own. The distinguishing feature is whether the molecular entity is a premodifier of a lexicalized disorder name or a post-nominal complement describing the mechanism. For Examples 2–4 the gold spans aren't shown, so whether gold has a shorter span or no entity at all is inferred. The rule below holds either way.\n\n### Step 2: Pattern Insight\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Post-nominal molecular \"deficiency of/in X\" 